speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, I have one other question and it also arises from the Auditor General's report. On page 39 of the report for the fiscal year ended March 31, 1966, there are a couple of paragraphs concerning admiralty courts. I should like to read a sentence or two starting with the second paragraph;
An examination during the year of all of the Admiralty courts disclosed a number of weaknesses in administration, including inadequate records, intermingling of federal and provincial funds, retention of the interest earnings by some of the registrars, fees improperly paid to the provinces, etc. Our report to the Department of Justice pointed out the need for more careful definition of the financial arrangements for these courts and for the establishment of adequate financial records. These matters are now under study by the accounting advisory service of the comptroller of the treasury.
I should like to ask the Solicitor General whether this matter is also being taken care of by the Department of Justice?

The house resumed from Wednesday, February 22, consideration of the motion of Mr. Mcllraith:
That this house do unite with the Senate in the appointment of a special joint committee to study and report upon amendments to the Criminal Code relating to the dissemination of varieties of "hate propaganda" in Canada as set out in Bill S-49, intituled: "An Act to amend the Criminal Code";
That 12 members of the House of Commons, to be designated by this house at a later date, be members of the said committee; and that standing order 67(1) be suspended in relation thereto;
That the committee have the power to call for persons, papers and records, to examine witnesses, to report from time to time, and to print such papers and evidence from day to day as may be ordered by the committee; and
That a message be sent to the Senate informing that house that the House of Commons do unite with the Senate for the above purposes.
Motion agreed to.
